<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import AddContact from './AddContact.abide'
import addContactSource from './AddContact.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`request()` is the real `Request`">
        <Code language="abide" caption="src/invoices/api.abide — excerpt" code={`<script server>
export const greeting = GET(() => {
    const locale = request().headers.get('accept-language') ?? 'en'
    return locale.startsWith('fr') ? 'Bonjour' : 'Hello'
})
</script>`} />

        <p>It is the platform's own <code>Request</code>, not a wrapper — so everything you already
        know about one applies, and anything that takes a <code>Request</code> takes this.</p>

        <p><strong>On an rpc route the body belongs to <code>ctx.args()</code>.</strong> A body
        reads once, so <code>request().json()</code> in a rung takes it and every rung after that
        one, and the handler, get nothing. That is the whole reason <code>ctx.args()</code> exists
        rather than being a convenience over the request: there is no second way in on that
        lane.</p>

        <p>Headers, the URL, the method and the signal are all fine to read. The body is the one
        part with an owner.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`response()` is the response being built">
        <Example
            id="response-created"
            title="`response()` — a status the handler decides"
            summary="An integration adds contacts through the API, and expects 201 and where the new record lives."
            files={{
                'AddContact.abide': addContactSource,
            }}
        >
            <AddContact />
        </Example>

        <p><em>0 response objects passed around</em> — the arm's route builds the status, the header
        and the body into one <code>Response</code>, so anything else wanting a header has to be
        handed it.</p>

        <p>It hands back the status and headers <strong>as they stand</strong>, before the body has
        been decided. That is what lets a rung deep in the stack contribute a header without
        becoming the thing that returns the response — the alternative is every layer passing a
        partial response outward and the outermost one assembling it.</p>

        <p>A handler that returns a whole <code>Response</code> decides its own;
        <code>response()</code> is for the case where you want to add to whatever the handler is
        going to send.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/answer-with-something-other-than-json')}>Response
        types</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`server()` is the listening server">
        <p>The <code>Bun.Server</code> the app is running on, typed by its
        <code>WebSocketData</code>. It is the escape hatch: a <code>server().upgrade()</code> for a
        protocol abide does not carry, <code>server().requestIP()</code> for a rate limiter that
        needs one.</p>

        <p>Reaching for it is a signal worth noticing. Most of what an app wants from the server is
        already a name: a live value is a followed <code>GET</code>, and an address is
        <code>rpc.url</code>. So a <code>server()</code> call is either something genuinely outside
        the framework or a name that has not been found yet.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Request-local means request-local">
        <p>Each of these is scoped to the request being served, and there is no moment at which one
        request's is visible to another's. That is not a convention to be careful about — it is what
        "ambient" means on a server, and it is the same scoping <code>principal</code>,
        <code>route</code> and <code>config</code> are under.</p>

        <p>In a browser there is no request, so <code>request()</code> and <code>response()</code>
        are not there to read. That is the one place these three differ from the ambients that
        answer on both sides.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/ambient-values')}>Ambient values</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> — the lane that
            reads these first</li>
            <li><a href={url('/app/know-who-is-calling')}>Auth &amp; principal</a> — who the request
            is from</li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/ambient-values')}>Ambient values</a> — the whole set, in
            one table</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
